1
00:00:02,750 --> 00:00:10,750
Chapter 12 The promise of a smooth career, which my first calm introduction to

2
00:00:10,751 --> 00:00:14,175
Thornfield Hall seemed to pledge, was not belied on

3
00:00:14,176 --> 00:00:16,889
a longer acquaintance with the place and its inmates.

4
00:00:18,689 --> 00:00:24,130
Mrs. Fairfax turned out to be what she appeared, a placid-tempered, kind-natured

5
00:00:24,131 --> 00:00:27,869
woman of competent education and average intelligence.

6
00:00:29,110 --> 00:00:33,051
My pupil was a lively child, who had been spoiled

7
00:00:33,052 --> 00:00:36,490
and indulged, and therefore was sometimes wayward.

8
00:00:37,759 --> 00:00:43,470
But as she was committed entirely to my care, and no injudicious interference from

9
00:00:43,471 --> 00:00:48,002
any quarter ever thwarted my plans for her improvement, she soon

10
00:00:48,003 --> 00:00:52,490
forgot her little freaks, and became obedient and teachable.

11
00:00:53,479 --> 00:00:59,290
She had no great talents, no marked traits of character, no peculiar development of

12
00:00:59,291 --> 00:01:05,010
feeling or taste, which raised her one inch above the ordinary level of childhood.

13
00:01:05,549 --> 00:01:09,389
But neither had she any deficiency or vice which sunk her below it.

14
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:13,787
She made reasonable progress entertained for me a

15
00:01:13,788 --> 00:01:17,490
vivacious, though perhaps not very profound affection.

16
00:01:19,220 --> 00:01:25,430
And by her simplicity, gay prattle and efforts to please inspired me in return,

17
00:01:26,049 --> 00:01:28,112
with a degree of attachment, sufficient to

18
00:01:28,113 --> 00:01:31,291
make us both content in each other's society.

19
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:37,650
This, paper and these, will be thought cool language by persons who entertain

20
00:01:37,651 --> 00:01:43,610
solemn doctrines about the angelic nature of children, and the duty of those charged

21
00:01:43,611 --> 00:01:48,389
with their education to conceive for them an idolatrous devotion.

22
00:01:50,119 --> 00:01:56,049
But I am not writing to flatter parental egotism, to echo cant or prop up humbug,

23
00:01:56,680 --> 00:01:58,590
I am merely telling the truth.

24
00:01:59,790 --> 00:02:03,174
I felt a conscientious solicitude for Adele's welfare

25
00:02:03,175 --> 00:02:06,189
and progress and a quiet liking for her little self.

26
00:02:07,049 --> 00:02:12,509
Just as I cherished towards Mrs. Fairfax a thankfulness for her kindness and a

27
00:02:12,510 --> 00:02:16,904
pleasure in her society, proportionate to the tranquil regards

28
00:02:16,905 --> 00:02:20,770
she had for me and the moderation of her mind and character.

29
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:26,909
Anybody may blame me who likes, when I add further, that now and then,

30
00:02:28,009 --> 00:02:33,229
when I took a walk by myself in the grounds, when I went down to the gates and

31
00:02:33,430 --> 00:02:38,990
looked through them along the road, or when, while Adele played with her nurse

32
00:02:38,991 --> 00:02:45,450
and Mrs. Fairfax made jellies in the store room, I climbed the three staircases,

33
00:02:46,310 --> 00:02:51,229
raised the trap door of the attic, and having reached the leads, looked out a

34
00:02:51,230 --> 00:02:58,790
far over sequestered field and hill, and a long dim skyline, that then I longed

35
00:02:58,791 --> 00:03:04,470
for a power of vision, which might overpass that limit, which might reach the

36
00:03:04,849 --> 00:03:12,849
busy world, towns, regions full of life I had heard of, but never seen, that then I

37
00:03:12,850 --> 00:03:18,590
desired more of practical experience than I possessed, more of intercourse with my

38
00:03:18,591 --> 00:03:24,330
kind, of acquaintance with variety of character, than was here within my reach.

39
00:03:25,389 --> 00:03:31,750
I valued what was good in Mrs. Fairfax, and what was good in Adele, but I believed

40
00:03:31,751 --> 00:03:37,209
in the existence of other and more vivid kinds of goodness, and what I believed in

41
00:03:37,210 --> 00:03:44,150
I wished to behold, who blames me, many no doubt, and I shall be called

42
00:03:44,151 --> 00:03:49,229
discontented, I could not help it, the restlessness was in my nature,

43
00:03:49,979 --> 00:03:56,450
it agitated me to pain sometimes, then my soul relief was to walk along the

44
00:03:56,451 --> 00:04:02,509
corridor of the third story, backwards and forwards, safe in the silence and solitude

45
00:04:02,510 --> 00:04:08,069
of the spot, and allow my mind's eye to dwell on whatever bright visions rose

46
00:04:08,070 --> 00:04:15,389
before it, and certainly, they were many and glowing, to let my heart be heaved by

47
00:04:15,390 --> 00:04:21,069
the exultant movement, which while it swelled it in trouble, expanded it with

48
00:04:21,070 --> 00:04:27,389
life, and best of all, to open my inward ear to a tale that was never ended,

49
00:04:28,189 --> 00:04:34,750
a tale my imagination created and narrated continuously, quickened with all of

50
00:04:34,751 --> 00:04:41,169
incident, life, fire, feeling that I desired and had not in my actual

51
00:04:41,170 --> 00:04:46,930
existence, it is in vain to say human beings ought to be satisfied with

52
00:04:46,931 --> 00:04:52,130
tranquility, they must have action, and they will make it if they cannot find

53
00:04:52,131 --> 00:04:58,669
it, millions are condemned to a stiller doom than mine, and millions are in silent

54
00:04:58,670 --> 00:05:04,849
revolt against their lot, nobody knows how many rebellions besides political

55
00:05:04,850 --> 00:05:11,630
rebellions ferment in the masses of life, which people earth, women are supposed to

56
00:05:11,631 --> 00:05:17,909
be very calm generally, but women feel just as men feel, they need exercise for

57
00:05:17,911 --> 00:05:23,290
their faculties, and a field for their efforts, as much as their brothers do,

58
00:05:24,169 --> 00:05:30,750
they suffer from too rigid a restraint, too absolute a stagnation, precisely as

59
00:05:30,751 --> 00:05:36,330
men would suffer, and it is narrow-minded in their more privileged fellow creatures,

60
00:05:37,110 --> 00:05:40,930
to say that they ought to confine themselves to making puddings and knitting

61
00:05:40,931 --> 00:05:47,569
stockings, to playing on the piano and embroidering bags, it is thoughtless to

62
00:05:47,570 --> 00:05:52,889
condemn them, or laugh at them, if they seek to do more, or learn more

63
00:05:52,890 --> 00:05:56,450
than custom has pronounced necessary for their sex.

64
00:05:58,169 --> 00:06:04,729
When thus alone, I not unfrequently heard Grace Poole's laugh, the same peel,

65
00:06:04,950 --> 00:06:10,889
the same low, slow, ha ha, which, when first heard, had thrilled me,

66
00:06:11,779 --> 00:06:17,349
I heard too her eccentric murmurs, stranger than her laugh, there were days

67
00:06:17,350 --> 00:06:20,131
when she was quite silent, but there were others

68
00:06:20,132 --> 00:06:23,049
when I could not account for the sounds she made.

69
00:06:24,310 --> 00:06:29,650
Sometimes I saw her, she would come out of her room with a basin, or a plate,

70
00:06:29,869 --> 00:06:35,069
or a tray in her hand, go down to the kitchen and shortly return, generally,

71
00:06:36,180 --> 00:06:41,470
or romantic reader, forgive me for telling the plain truth, bearing a pot of porter.

72
00:06:42,669 --> 00:06:48,069
Her appearance always acted as a damper to the curiosity raised by her oral oddities.

73
00:06:49,020 --> 00:06:54,049
Hard featured and stayed, she had no point to which interest could attach.

74
00:06:55,110 --> 00:07:00,189
I made some attempts to draw her into conversation, but she seemed a person of

75
00:07:00,190 --> 00:07:07,510
few words, a monosyllabic reply, usually cut short every effort of that sort.

76
00:07:07,799 --> 00:07:13,470
The other members of the household, Viz, John and his wife, Lea, the

77
00:07:13,471 --> 00:07:16,970
housemaid, and Sophie, the French nurse, were

78
00:07:16,971 --> 00:07:20,530
decent people, but in no respect remarkable.

79
00:07:21,490 --> 00:07:26,470
With Sophie, I used to talk French, and sometimes I asked her questions about

80
00:07:26,471 --> 00:07:32,189
her native country, but she was not of a descriptive or narrative turn and

81
00:07:32,190 --> 00:07:35,475
generally gave such vapid and confused answers as were

82
00:07:35,476 --> 00:07:39,229
calculated, rather to check, then encourage inquiry.

83
00:07:40,389 --> 00:07:43,990
October, November, December, passed away.

84
00:07:45,229 --> 00:07:48,445
One afternoon in January, Mrs. Fairfax had begged

85
00:07:48,447 --> 00:07:51,690
a holiday for Adele, because she had a cold.

86
00:07:52,540 --> 00:07:58,090
And, as Adele seconded the request with an order that reminded me how precious

87
00:07:58,091 --> 00:08:03,810
occasional holidays had been to me in my own childhood, I accorded it, deeming that

88
00:08:03,811 --> 00:08:06,250
I did well and showing pliability on the point.

89
00:08:07,529 --> 00:08:11,090
It was a fine, calm day, though very cold.

90
00:08:12,029 --> 00:08:15,830
I was tired of sitting still in the library through a whole long morning.

91
00:08:17,009 --> 00:08:20,889
Mrs. Fairfax had just written a letter, which was waiting to be posted,

92
00:08:21,694 --> 00:08:26,250
so I put on my bonnet and cloak, and volunteered to carry it to hay.

93
00:08:27,029 --> 00:08:31,590
The distance, two miles, would be a pleasant winter afternoon walk.

94
00:08:33,020 --> 00:08:38,450
Having seen Adele comfortably seated in her little chair by Mrs. Fairfax's parlor

95
00:08:38,451 --> 00:08:43,950
fireside, and given her her best wax doll, which I usually kept enveloped in silver

96
00:08:43,951 --> 00:08:48,369
paper in a drawer, to play with, and a storybook for change of amusement,

97
00:08:49,369 --> 00:08:54,930
and having replied to her, Revene bientôt, Marbon Ami, Maché Mazzell Jeanette,

98
00:08:55,610 --> 00:08:57,029
with a kiss I set out.

99
00:08:57,849 --> 00:09:04,450
The ground was hard, the air was still, my road was lonely, I walked fast till I

100
00:09:04,451 --> 00:09:08,524
got warm, and then I walked slowly to enjoy and analyze the

101
00:09:08,623 --> 00:09:12,830
species of pleasure brooding for me in the hour and situation.

102
00:09:14,240 --> 00:09:15,240
It was three o'clock.

103
00:09:16,009 --> 00:09:21,610
The church bell told as I passed under the bell-free, the charm of the hour lay in

104
00:09:21,611 --> 00:09:26,549
its approaching dimness, in the low gliding and pale beaming sun.

105
00:09:27,939 --> 00:09:32,590
I was a mile from Thornfield, in a lane noted for wild roses in summer,

106
00:09:33,189 --> 00:09:37,830
for nuts and blackberries in autumn, and even now possessing a few coral

107
00:09:37,830 --> 00:09:41,417
treasures in hips and hares, but whose best winter

108
00:09:41,418 --> 00:09:45,689
delight lay in its utter solitude and leafless repose.

109
00:09:47,009 --> 00:09:52,310
If a breath of air stirred, it made no sound here, for there was not a holly,

110
00:09:52,930 --> 00:09:58,130
not an ever green to rustle, and the stripped hawthorn and hazelbushes were as

111
00:09:58,131 --> 00:10:03,491
still as the white, worn stones, which cause weighed the middle of the path.

112
00:10:03,919 --> 00:10:09,549
Far and wide on each side, there were only fields where no cattle now browsed,

113
00:10:10,270 --> 00:10:15,090
and the little brown birds, which stirred occasionally in the hedge, looked like

114
00:10:15,091 --> 00:10:17,810
single, russet leaves that had forgotten to drop.

115
00:10:19,529 --> 00:10:23,889
This lane inclined uphill all the way to hay.

116
00:10:25,270 --> 00:10:30,470
Having reached the middle, I sat down on a style which led thence into a field,

117
00:10:31,659 --> 00:10:35,430
gathering my mantle about me and sheltering my hands in my muff.

118
00:10:36,360 --> 00:10:42,049
I did not feel the cold, though it froze keenly, as was attested by a sheet of ice

119
00:10:42,050 --> 00:10:46,156
covering the causeway, where a little brooklet, now

120
00:10:46,157 --> 00:10:50,090
congealed, had overflowed after a rapid thaw some days since.

121
00:10:51,240 --> 00:10:54,470
From my seat, I could look down on thornfield.

122
00:10:55,340 --> 00:11:00,310
The grey and battle-minted hall was the principal object in the veil below me.

123
00:11:01,349 --> 00:11:04,189
Its woods and dark ruckery rose against the west.

124
00:11:05,159 --> 00:11:08,302
I lingered till the sun went down amongst the

125
00:11:08,304 --> 00:11:11,690
trees, and sank crimson and clear behind them.

126
00:11:12,209 --> 00:11:13,770
I then turned eastward.

127
00:11:14,610 --> 00:11:17,770
On the hilltop above me sat the rising moon.

128
00:11:19,330 --> 00:11:24,729
Pale yet as a cloud, but brightening momentarily, she looked over hay which,

129
00:11:24,979 --> 00:11:28,630
half-lost in trees, sent up a blue smoke from its few chimneys.

130
00:11:29,540 --> 00:11:32,773
It was yet a mile distant, but in the absolute

131
00:11:32,774 --> 00:11:36,330
hush I could hear plainly its thin murmurs of life.

132
00:11:37,279 --> 00:11:42,310
My ear too felt the flow of currents, in what dails and depths I could not tell.

133
00:11:43,555 --> 00:11:49,651
But there were many hills beyond hay, and outless many becks threading their passes.

134
00:11:50,430 --> 00:11:55,250
That evening, calm betrayed alike the tinkle of the nearest streams,

135
00:11:55,970 --> 00:11:57,590
the sour of the most remote.

136
00:11:59,330 --> 00:12:02,841
A rude noise broke on these fine ripplings and

137
00:12:02,842 --> 00:12:06,771
whisperings, at once so far away and so clear.

138
00:12:07,419 --> 00:12:14,070
A positive tramp, tramp, a metallic clatter which effaced the soft wave wanderings.

139
00:12:14,630 --> 00:12:20,029
As, in a picture, the solid mass of a crag, or the rough bowls of a great oak,

140
00:12:20,790 --> 00:12:25,830
drawn in dark and strong on the foreground, effaced the aerial distance of

141
00:12:25,830 --> 00:12:32,150
azure hill, sunny horizon, and blended clouds where tint melts into tint.

142
00:12:33,189 --> 00:12:38,750
The din was on the causeway, a horse was coming, the windings of the lane yet hid

143
00:12:38,950 --> 00:12:40,490
it, but it approached.

144
00:12:41,540 --> 00:12:43,369
I was just leaving the style.

145
00:12:44,430 --> 00:12:49,110
Yet, as the path was narrow, I sat still to let it go by.

146
00:12:50,209 --> 00:12:55,430
In those days I was young, and all sorts of fancies bright and dark tenanted my

147
00:12:55,431 --> 00:13:00,110
mind, the memories of nursery stories were amongst other rubbish.

148
00:13:01,069 --> 00:13:05,598
And when they recurred, maturing youth added to them a

149
00:13:05,599 --> 00:13:09,110
vigor and vividness beyond what childhood could give.

150
00:13:10,750 --> 00:13:15,729
As this horse approached, and as I watched for it to appear through the dusk,

151
00:13:16,619 --> 00:13:21,669
I remembered certain of Bessie's tales, wherein figured a north of England's

152
00:13:21,670 --> 00:13:28,029
spirit called a guitrash, which in the form of horse, mule or large dog,

153
00:13:28,590 --> 00:13:32,716
haunted solitary ways, and sometimes came upon belated

154
00:13:32,717 --> 00:13:35,849
travellers, as this horse was now coming upon me.

155
00:13:36,819 --> 00:13:39,209
It was very near, but not yet in sight.

156
00:13:40,159 --> 00:13:45,970
When, in addition to the tramp, tramp, I heard a rush under the hedge,

157
00:13:46,709 --> 00:13:52,450
and close down by the hazel stems, glided a great dog whose black and white

158
00:13:52,451 --> 00:13:55,029
colour made him a distinct object against the trees.

159
00:13:56,174 --> 00:13:59,723
It was exactly one form of Bessie's guitrash, a

160
00:13:59,724 --> 00:14:03,909
lion-like creature with long hair and a huge head.

161
00:14:04,880 --> 00:14:10,750
It passed me, however, quietly enough, not staying to look up with strange

162
00:14:10,751 --> 00:14:14,869
pretercanon eyes in my face, as I half expected it would.

163
00:14:15,994 --> 00:14:20,470
The horse followed, a tall steed, and on its back a rider.

164
00:14:21,479 --> 00:14:25,209
The man, the human being, broke the spell at once.

165
00:14:26,270 --> 00:14:30,970
Nothing ever rode the guitrash, it was always alone, and goblins to my

166
00:14:30,971 --> 00:14:34,832
notions, though they might tenant the dumb carcasses of beasts,

167
00:14:34,833 --> 00:14:38,270
could scarce covet shelter in the commonplace human form.

168
00:14:39,345 --> 00:14:44,590
No guitrash was this, only a traveller taking the shortcut to Milkut.

169
00:14:45,540 --> 00:14:52,409
He passed, and I went on, a few steps, and I turned, a sliding sound and an

170
00:14:52,411 --> 00:14:55,883
exclamation of what the deuce is to do now,

171
00:14:55,883 --> 00:14:59,811
and a clattering tumble arrested my attention.

172
00:15:00,169 --> 00:15:01,549
Man and horse were down.

173
00:15:02,369 --> 00:15:05,470
They had slipped on the sheet of ice, which glazed the causeway.

174
00:15:06,189 --> 00:15:11,389
The dog came bounding back and seeing his master in a predicament, and hearing the

175
00:15:11,390 --> 00:15:16,120
horse grown, barked till the evening hills echoed the

176
00:15:16,121 --> 00:15:20,069
sound, which was deep in proportion to his magnitude.

177
00:15:21,639 --> 00:15:25,590
He snuffed around the prostrate group, and then he ran up to me.

178
00:15:26,470 --> 00:15:27,549
It was all he could do.

179
00:15:28,294 --> 00:15:30,409
There was no other help at hand to summon.

180
00:15:31,470 --> 00:15:34,863
I obeyed him, and walked down to the traveller by

181
00:15:34,864 --> 00:15:38,590
this time struggling himself free of his steed.

182
00:15:40,279 --> 00:15:43,560
His efforts were so vigorous, I thought he could

183
00:15:43,561 --> 00:15:46,870
not be much hurt, but I asked him the question.

184
00:15:47,639 --> 00:15:48,790
Are you injured, sir?

185
00:15:50,090 --> 00:15:52,669
I think he was swearing, but I'm not certain.

186
00:15:53,310 --> 00:15:55,729
However, he was pronouncing some formula which

187
00:15:55,730 --> 00:15:58,771
prevented him from replying to me directly.

188
00:16:00,270 --> 00:16:01,270
Can I do anything?

189
00:16:01,970 --> 00:16:02,970
I asked again.

190
00:16:03,630 --> 00:16:05,450
You must just stand on one side.

191
00:16:06,189 --> 00:16:10,110
He answered as he rose, first to his knees, and then to his feet.

192
00:16:10,810 --> 00:16:11,810
I did.

193
00:16:12,389 --> 00:16:17,349
Whereupon began a heaving, stamping, clattering process, accompanied by a

194
00:16:17,350 --> 00:16:21,650
barking and baying, which removed me effectively some yard's distance.

195
00:16:22,770 --> 00:16:25,830
But I would not be driven quite away until I saw the event.

196
00:16:26,840 --> 00:16:28,669
This was finally fortunate.

197
00:16:29,610 --> 00:16:35,029
The horse was reestablished, and the dog was silenced with a down pilot.

198
00:16:36,189 --> 00:16:41,150
The traveller now stooping felt his foot and leg as if trying whether they were

199
00:16:41,151 --> 00:16:45,028
sound, apparently something ailed them, for he halted

200
00:16:45,029 --> 00:16:48,909
to the style whence I had just risen and sat down.

201
00:16:50,360 --> 00:16:54,229
I was in the mood for being useful, or at least a fissious, I think,

202
00:16:54,990 --> 00:16:56,490
for I now drew near him again.

203
00:16:58,270 --> 00:17:01,062
If you are hurt and want help, sir, I can fetch

204
00:17:01,063 --> 00:17:03,930
someone either from Thornfield Hall or from Hay.

205
00:17:05,310 --> 00:17:06,310
Thank you.

206
00:17:06,669 --> 00:17:07,669
I shall do.

207
00:17:07,790 --> 00:17:10,490
I have no broken bones, only a sprain.

208
00:17:11,319 --> 00:17:17,110
And again, he stood up and tried his foot, but the result extorted and involuntary.

209
00:17:17,450 --> 00:17:18,450
Uch!

210
00:17:19,290 --> 00:17:24,069
Something of daylight still lingered, and the moon was waxing bright.

211
00:17:25,419 --> 00:17:26,689
I could see him plainly.

212
00:17:27,849 --> 00:17:33,110
His figure was enveloped in a riding cloak, fur collared and steel clasped.

213
00:17:34,060 --> 00:17:37,422
Its details were not apparent, but I traced the general

214
00:17:37,423 --> 00:17:40,470
points of middle height and considerable breadth of chest.

215
00:17:41,569 --> 00:17:45,909
He had a dark face, with stern features and a heavy brow.

216
00:17:46,650 --> 00:17:51,270
His eyes and gathered eyebrows looked irefull and thwarted just now.

217
00:17:52,180 --> 00:17:55,770
He was past youth, but had not reached middle age.

218
00:17:56,430 --> 00:17:57,970
Perhaps he might be 35.

219
00:17:59,639 --> 00:18:02,630
I felt no fear of him, and but little shyness.

220
00:18:03,930 --> 00:18:09,849
Had he been a handsome, heroic looking young gentleman, I should not have dared

221
00:18:09,850 --> 00:18:15,229
to stand thus questioning him against his will and offering my services unasked.

222
00:18:16,139 --> 00:18:21,090
I had hardly ever seen a handsome youth never in my life spoken to one.

223
00:18:21,869 --> 00:18:26,729
I had a theoretical reverence and homage for beauty, elegance, gallantry,

224
00:18:27,389 --> 00:18:28,389
fascination.

225
00:18:29,509 --> 00:18:34,790
But had I met those qualities incarnate in masculine shape, I should have known

226
00:18:34,791 --> 00:18:40,630
instinctively that they neither had nor could have sympathy with anything in me,

227
00:18:41,580 --> 00:18:45,600
and should have shunned them as one would fire, lightning,

228
00:18:45,601 --> 00:18:49,470
or anything else that is bright but antipathetic.

229
00:18:51,029 --> 00:18:56,549
If even this stranger had smiled and been good-humoured to me when I addressed him.

230
00:18:58,389 --> 00:19:03,189
If he had put off my offer of assistance galley and with thanks, I should have gone

231
00:19:03,190 --> 00:19:07,369
on my way and not felt any vocation to renew inquiries.

232
00:19:08,540 --> 00:19:12,630
But the frown, the roughness of the traveller, set me at my ease.

233
00:19:13,880 --> 00:19:19,750
I retained my station when he waived to me to go, and announced, I cannot think of

234
00:19:19,751 --> 00:19:23,932
leaving you, sir, at so late an hour in this solitary

235
00:19:23,981 --> 00:19:26,930
lane till I see you are fit to mount your horse.

236
00:19:28,250 --> 00:19:29,770
He looked at me when I said this.

237
00:19:30,819 --> 00:19:33,450
He had hardly turned his eyes in my direction before.

238
00:19:34,729 --> 00:19:38,209
I should think you ought to be at home yourself," said he.

239
00:19:38,990 --> 00:19:42,549
If you have a home in this neighbourhood, where do you come from?

240
00:19:43,650 --> 00:19:44,650
From just below.

241
00:19:46,055 --> 00:19:49,549
And I am not at all afraid of being out late when it is moonlight.

242
00:19:50,639 --> 00:19:53,750
I will run over to hay for you with pleasure, if you wish it.

243
00:19:54,290 --> 00:19:56,950
Indeed, I'm going there to post a letter.

244
00:19:58,709 --> 00:19:59,750
You live just below?

245
00:20:04,909 --> 00:20:12,909
Do you mean at that house with the stinked and pale from the woods that, by contrast

246
00:20:12,911 --> 00:20:16,729
with the western sky, now seemed one mass of shadow?

247
00:20:18,029 --> 00:20:19,029
Yes, sir.

248
00:20:19,830 --> 00:20:22,110
Whose house is it, Mr. Rochester's?

249
00:20:23,220 --> 00:20:24,259
Do you know Mr. Rochester?

250
00:20:25,259 --> 00:20:26,930
No, I have never seen him.

251
00:20:28,020 --> 00:20:29,430
He is not resident, then.

252
00:20:30,150 --> 00:20:31,150
No.

253
00:20:31,229 --> 00:20:32,349
Can you tell me where he is?

254
00:20:32,869 --> 00:20:33,869
I cannot.

255
00:20:34,250 --> 00:20:38,029
You are not a servant at the stop.

256
00:20:39,060 --> 00:20:43,110
Ran his eye over my dress, which, as usual, was quite simple.

257
00:20:43,869 --> 00:20:46,948
A black marino cloak, a black beaver bonnet,

258
00:20:46,949 --> 00:20:50,050
neither of them half fine enough for a lady's maid.

259
00:20:50,770 --> 00:20:52,849
He seemed puzzled to decide what I was.

260
00:20:53,430 --> 00:20:54,430
I helped him.

261
00:20:55,169 --> 00:20:56,169
I am the governess.

262
00:20:57,150 --> 00:20:59,549
Ah, the governess, he repeated.

263
00:21:00,189 --> 00:21:01,869
Doos take me if I had not forgotten.

264
00:21:02,389 --> 00:21:03,389
The governess.

265
00:21:04,145 --> 00:21:06,650
And again, my raiment underwent scrutiny.

266
00:21:07,529 --> 00:21:13,251
In two minutes he rose from the style, his face expressed pain when he tried to move.

267
00:21:14,185 --> 00:21:17,829
I cannot commission you to fetch help, he said, but you

268
00:21:17,830 --> 00:21:20,790
may help me a little yourself if you will be so kind.

269
00:21:21,909 --> 00:21:22,909
Yes, sir.

270
00:21:23,689 --> 00:21:26,049
You have not an umbrella that I can use as a stick?

271
00:21:27,029 --> 00:21:28,029
No.

272
00:21:28,639 --> 00:21:31,790
Try to get hold of my horse's bridal and lead him to me.

273
00:21:32,580 --> 00:21:34,090
You are not afraid?

274
00:21:36,125 --> 00:21:40,569
I should have been afraid to touch a horse when alone, but when told to do it,

275
00:21:40,950 --> 00:21:42,590
I was disposed to obey.

276
00:21:44,584 --> 00:21:48,430
I put down my muff on the style and went up to the tall steed.

277
00:21:49,264 --> 00:21:52,394
I endeavoured to catch the bridal, but it was a

278
00:21:52,395 --> 00:21:55,490
spirited thing and would not let me come near its head.

279
00:21:56,139 --> 00:21:59,330
I made effort on effort, though in vain.

280
00:22:00,270 --> 00:22:04,610
Meantime, I was mortally afraid of its trampling four feet.

281
00:22:05,919 --> 00:22:11,669
The traveller waited and watched for some time, and at last he laughed, I see.

282
00:22:12,450 --> 00:22:15,610
He said, the mountain will never be brought to Muhammad.

283
00:22:16,430 --> 00:22:19,610
So all you can do is to aid Muhammad to go to the mountain.

284
00:22:20,250 --> 00:22:21,790
I must beg of you to come here.

285
00:22:22,669 --> 00:22:23,669
I came.

286
00:22:24,720 --> 00:22:29,790
Excuse me, he continued, necessity compels me to make you useful.

287
00:22:31,069 --> 00:22:35,310
He laid a heavy hand on my shoulder, and leaning on me with some stress,

288
00:22:35,990 --> 00:22:36,990
limped to his horse.

289
00:22:37,830 --> 00:22:41,709
Having once caught the bridal, he mastered it directly and sprang to his

290
00:22:41,710 --> 00:22:46,229
saddle, grimacing grimly as he made the effort for it wrenched his sprain.

291
00:22:47,909 --> 00:22:54,409
Now, said he, releasing his underlip from a hard bite, just hand me my whip.

292
00:22:54,869 --> 00:22:56,150
It lies there under the hedge.

293
00:22:57,229 --> 00:22:59,209
I sought it and found it.

294
00:22:59,549 --> 00:23:00,549
Thank you.

295
00:23:00,810 --> 00:23:04,590
Now make haste with the letter to hey and return as fast as you can.

296
00:23:06,154 --> 00:23:11,689
A touch of a spurred heel made his horse first start and rear and then bound away.

297
00:23:12,650 --> 00:23:17,346
The dog rushed in his traces, all three vanished, like

298
00:23:17,347 --> 00:23:22,590
heathed that in the wilderness, the wild wind whirls away.

299
00:23:24,319 --> 00:23:26,650
I took up my moth and walked on.

300
00:23:27,889 --> 00:23:30,470
The incident had occurred and was gone for me.

301
00:23:31,290 --> 00:23:36,229
It was an incident of no moment, no romance, no interest in a sense,

302
00:23:37,009 --> 00:23:41,650
yet it marked with change one single hour of a monotonous life.

303
00:23:42,419 --> 00:23:44,310
My help had been needed and claimed.

304
00:23:44,990 --> 00:23:45,990
I had given it.

305
00:23:46,270 --> 00:23:47,810
I was pleased to have done something.

306
00:23:48,470 --> 00:23:50,869
Trivial, transitory, though the deed was.

307
00:23:51,799 --> 00:23:56,110
It was yet an active thing and I was weary of an existence all passive.

308
00:23:57,595 --> 00:24:04,310
The new face too was like a new picture introduced to the gallery of memory and it

309
00:24:04,311 --> 00:24:06,349
was dissimilar to all the others hanging there.

310
00:24:07,349 --> 00:24:14,651
Firstly, because it was masculine and secondly because it was dark, strong and stern.

311
00:24:16,569 --> 00:24:18,842
I had it still before me when I entered hay

312
00:24:18,843 --> 00:24:22,170
and slipped the letter into the post office.

313
00:24:22,770 --> 00:24:26,610
I saw it as I walked fast downhill all the way home.

314
00:24:28,360 --> 00:24:33,209
When I came to the style, I stopped a minute, looked round and listened.

315
00:24:34,259 --> 00:24:38,990
With an idea that a horse's hoofs might ring on the causeway again, and that a

316
00:24:38,991 --> 00:24:43,650
rider in a cloak and a getrish like Newfoundland dog might be again apparent.

317
00:24:44,650 --> 00:24:48,858
I saw only the hedge and a Pollard Willow before me,

318
00:24:48,859 --> 00:24:52,111
rising up still and straight to meet the moon beams.

319
00:24:53,340 --> 00:24:57,062
I heard only the faintest waft of wind roaming fitful

320
00:24:57,063 --> 00:25:00,950
among the trees around Thornfield, a mile distant.

321
00:25:02,310 --> 00:25:07,590
And when I glanced down in the direction of the murmur, my eye traversing the

322
00:25:07,591 --> 00:25:10,830
hallfront caught a light kindling in a window.

323
00:25:11,979 --> 00:25:15,130
It reminded me that I was late and I hurried on.

324
00:25:16,119 --> 00:25:18,229
I did not like re-entering Thornfield.

325
00:25:19,090 --> 00:25:24,270
To pass its threshold was to return to stagnation, to cross the silent hall,

326
00:25:24,990 --> 00:25:29,569
to ascend the darksome staircase, to seek my own lonely little room,

327
00:25:30,279 --> 00:25:36,729
and then to meet Tranquil Mrs. Fairfax and spend the long winter evening with her and

328
00:25:36,730 --> 00:25:42,369
her only was to quail wholly the faint excitement wakened by my walk.

329
00:25:43,319 --> 00:25:46,667
To slip again over my faculties, the viewless

330
00:25:46,668 --> 00:25:50,310
fetters of an uniform and to still existence.

331
00:25:51,229 --> 00:25:54,414
Of an existence whose very privileges of security

332
00:25:54,415 --> 00:25:58,229
and ease I was becoming incapable of appreciating.

333
00:25:59,779 --> 00:26:04,590
What good it would have done me at that time, to have been tossed in the storms of

334
00:26:04,591 --> 00:26:09,830
an uncertain struggling life, and to have been taught by rough and bitter

335
00:26:09,830 --> 00:26:15,250
experience, to long for the calm amidst which I now repined.

336
00:26:16,590 --> 00:26:22,470
Yes, just as much good as it would do a man tired of sitting still in a too easy

337
00:26:22,471 --> 00:26:27,546
chair to take a long walk, and just as natural was the wish

338
00:26:27,547 --> 00:26:32,009
to stir, under my circumstances, as it would be under his.

339
00:26:33,560 --> 00:26:39,409
I lingered at the gates, I lingered on the lawn, I paced backwards and forwards on

340
00:26:39,411 --> 00:26:44,970
the pavement, the shutters of the glass door were closed, I could not see into the

341
00:26:44,971 --> 00:26:50,450
interior, and both my eyes and spirit seemed drawn from the gloomy house,

342
00:26:51,169 --> 00:26:54,241
from the gray hollow filled with railer cells as

343
00:26:54,242 --> 00:26:58,229
it appeared to me, to that sky expanded before me.

344
00:26:58,990 --> 00:27:05,150
A blue sea absolved from taint of cloud, the moon ascending it in solemn march,

345
00:27:06,110 --> 00:27:11,430
her orb seeming to look up as she left the hilltops, from behind which she had come,

346
00:27:12,189 --> 00:27:18,369
far and farther below her, and aspired to the zenith, midnight dark in its

347
00:27:18,370 --> 00:27:23,610
fathomless depth and measureless distance, and for those trembling stars that

348
00:27:23,611 --> 00:27:29,630
followed her course, they made my heart tremble, my veins glow when I viewed them.

349
00:27:30,590 --> 00:27:35,330
Little things recall us to earth, the clock struck in the hall, that's a

350
00:27:35,330 --> 00:27:40,250
feist, I turned from moon and stars, opened a side door and went in.

351
00:27:41,520 --> 00:27:46,889
The hall was not dark, nor yet was it lit, only by the high-hung bronze lamp,

352
00:27:47,610 --> 00:27:52,290
a warm glow suffused both it and the lower steps of the oak staircase.

353
00:27:53,709 --> 00:27:58,930
This ruddy shine issued from the great dining-room whose two-leaved door stood

354
00:27:58,931 --> 00:28:05,669
open and showed a genial fire in the great, glancing on marble hearth and brass

355
00:28:05,670 --> 00:28:09,356
fire ions, and revealing purple draperies and

356
00:28:09,358 --> 00:28:13,290
polished furniture, in the most pleasant radiance.

357
00:28:14,485 --> 00:28:20,250
It revealed, too, a group near the mantelpiece, I had scarcely caught it,

358
00:28:21,009 --> 00:28:26,069
and scarcely become aware of a cheerful mingling of voices, amongst which I seemed

359
00:28:26,070 --> 00:28:29,590
to distinguish the tones of a del when the door closed.

360
00:28:30,849 --> 00:28:34,480
I hastened to Mrs Fairfax's room, there was a

361
00:28:34,481 --> 00:28:38,389
fire there, too, but no candle and no Mrs Fairfax.

362
00:28:39,439 --> 00:28:45,830
Instead, all alone, sitting upright on the rug, and gazing with gravity at the blaze,

363
00:28:46,470 --> 00:28:53,131
I beheld a great black and white long -haired dog just like the getrash of the lane.

364
00:28:53,430 --> 00:28:57,470
It was so like it that I went forward and said pilot,

365
00:28:57,471 --> 00:29:01,630
and the thing got up and came to me and snuffed me.

366
00:29:02,560 --> 00:29:08,630
I caressed him and he wagged his great tail, but he looked an eerie creature to

367
00:29:08,631 --> 00:29:11,950
be alone with, and I could not tell when she had come.

368
00:29:12,909 --> 00:29:15,637
I rang the bell for I wanted a candle, and I

369
00:29:15,638 --> 00:29:18,751
wanted, too, to get an account of this visitant.

370
00:29:19,290 --> 00:29:20,290
Lear entered.

371
00:29:20,529 --> 00:29:21,529
What dog is this?

372
00:29:22,610 --> 00:29:24,970
He came with master, with whom?

373
00:29:25,889 --> 00:29:28,330
With master, Mr. Rochester, he has just arrived.

374
00:29:29,360 --> 00:29:31,770
Indeed, and is Mrs Fairfax with him?

375
00:29:32,750 --> 00:29:38,529
Yes, and Mr. Dell, they are in the dining room, and John is gone for a surgeon.

376
00:29:39,369 --> 00:29:44,349
For master has had an accident, his horse fell and his ankle is sprained.

377
00:29:45,430 --> 00:29:46,709
Did the horse fall in hay lane?

378
00:29:47,779 --> 00:29:51,130
Yes, coming downhill, it slipped on some ice.

379
00:29:52,270 --> 00:29:54,450
Ah, bring me a candle, will you Lear?

380
00:29:55,880 --> 00:29:56,930
Lear brought it.

381
00:29:57,590 --> 00:30:05,189
She entered, followed by Mrs. Fairfax, who repeated the news, adding that Mr.

382
00:30:05,349 --> 00:30:09,669
Carter the surgeon was come and was now with Mr. Rochester.

383
00:30:11,250 --> 00:30:15,322
Then she hurried out to give orders about tea,

384
00:30:15,323 --> 00:30:19,550
and I went upstairs to take off my things.
